== 11. Of the Husband

Married folks are either husband or wife.

The husband is he who has authority over the wife. For this reason Scripture calls him “the guide of her youth” (Prov 2:17). And since the two of them are but one flesh, he is also the head over his #emph[wife.]

The duties of the husband toward the wife are these:

#emph[I. To love her as himself:] Eph 5:33: “Each one of you#super[+] also must love his wife as he loves himself.” Gen 24:67: “And Isaac brought her into the tent of his mother Sarah and took Rebekah as his wife. And Isaac loved her and was comforted after his mother’s death.” #emph[Note how the husband’s love for his wife eases his sorrow for the death of his mother.]

#emph[He is to show this love in two things. First, in protecting her from danger,] Gen 20:16: “And he said to Sarah, ‘See, I am giving your brother a thousand pieces of silver. He is a covering of the eyes for you before all who are with you; you are completely cleared.’” 1 Sam 30:5: “David’s two wives, Ahinoam of Jezreel and Abigail the widow of Nabal of Carmel, had been taken captive.” v. 8: “David inquired of the #smallcaps[Lord]: ‘Should I pursue these raiders? Will I overtake them?’ ‘Pursue them,’ the #smallcaps[Lord] replied, ‘for you will surely overtake them and rescue the captives.’” #emph[Secondly, in regarding her welfare as his own, and providing maintenance for her, both for his lifetime and, as far as he can, for the time to come after his death.] Eph 5:28: “Husbands ought to love their wives as their own bodies. He who loves his wife loves himself.” v. 29: “No one ever hated his own flesh, but he nourishes … it,” and so on. Exod 21:10: “If he takes another wife, he must not reduce the food, clothing, or marital rights of his first wife.” Ruth 3:9: “‘Who are you?’ he asked. ‘I am your servant Ruth,’ she replied. ‘Spread the corner of your garment over me, for you are a kinsman-redeemer.’”

II. To honor his wife: 1 Pet 3:7: “… giving honor to the woman …”

This honor stands in three things. First, in esteeming her as his companion, or yoke-fellow. For this reason the woman, when she was created, was not taken out of the man’s head, because she was not made to rule over him; nor out of his feet, because God did not make her subject to him as a servant; but out of his side, so that the man should take her as his mate. Secondly, in a wise and patient bearing or covering of her infirmities, such as anger, waywardness and the like, in respect of the weakness of her sex. 1 Pet 3:7: “… live with your#super[+] wives with consideration, giving honor to the woman as the weaker vessel and as fellow heirs of the gracious gift of life, so that your#super[+] prayers will not be hindered.” Thirdly, by allowing himself at times to be admonished or advised by her. It was God’s commandment to Abraham concerning his wife Sarah: “Do not be distressed about the boy and your maidservant. Listen to everything that Sarah tells you, for through Isaac your offspring will be reckoned” (Gen 21:12). So Elkanah was willing to agree to his wife Hannah’s advice that she stay at home till the child was weaned, 1 Sam 1:23: “‘Do what you think is best,’ her husband Elkanah replied, ‘and stay here until you have weaned him. Only may the #smallcaps[Lord] confirm His word.’” For this reason the heathen philosopher said,#footnote[Arist. Ethic. lib. 8.] “The master of the family exercises (after a sort) a tyrannical power over his servants, and a royal power over his children, because kings are fathers of their commonwealths; but toward his wife he exercises an aristocratic power, not according to his own will, but agreeable to the honor and dignity of the married state.” And so, he said, the husband ought not in modesty to claim the privilege of directing and advising his wife in all household matters, but in some to leave her to her own will and judgment.

Here the question is raised whether the husband may punish his wife.

#emph[Answer.] Though the husband is the wife’s head, yet it seems that he has no power or liberty granted him in this respect. For we do not read in the Scriptures any precept or example to warrant such a use of his authority. He may reprove and admonish her in words only, if he sees her at fault. For so we read that Jacob rebuked his wife when she was impatient, and that even in anger, Gen 30:2: “Am I in the place of God, who has withheld children from you?” And Job rebuked his wife, Job 2:10: “You speak as a foolish woman speaks. … Should we accept from God only good and not adversity?” But he may not chastise her, either with lashes or with blows. The reason is plain: wives are their husbands’ mates, and the two of them are one flesh. And no man will hate, much less beat, his own flesh, but nourishes and cherishes it, Eph 5:29. Again, it is God’s commandment that a man should not trespass against the wife of his youth, Mal 2:15. Chrysostom says,#footnote[In 1 Cor 11. Homil. 26.] “It is the greatest reproach that can be for any man to beat his wife.” Plutarch, in the life of Cato the Censor, affirms that “he who strikes his wife” does the same “as if he should lay violent hands on the sacred images of the gods”; and this was counted a high degree of offense among the heathen.

It is alleged that husbands are commanded to love their wives as Christ loves his church; now Christ chastises his church with blows; and therefore the husband may do so to his wife. #emph[Answer.] As Christ loves his church entirely, so he may also chastise it, because he is not only its husband but also its absolute Lord and King. But the husband is not so absolute over the wife.

But, it is said, his authority over the wife is in a sort civil, as is the authority of the magistrate over his people. #emph[Answer.] It is not so. For the magistrate has the power of the sword in his hand, and by that power he inflicts punishment for offenses. But the husband can claim no such power for himself.#footnote[L. Consensu. C. de diuortijs.] Indeed, it is flatly forbidden in the civil law that he should scourge or strike his wife.

Nevertheless, if she grows to extremities and is desperately perverse, so that there is no hope of amendment, then the magistrate may be informed. To prevent scandals and to provide for public peace, the magistrate both ought and may assign to her necessary correction and punishment according to what she deserves. Now the husband who has so stubborn and contrary a wife must bear it, if it can be borne, as the portion of his cross laid on him by God. And in this case, if he is impatient, he may in some sort be pardoned and pitied, but he is not wholly to be excused.
